course: dictionary
lesson: 14960
title: Giving something up
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Relinquished means gave up or let go of something. You can relinquish power, a right or control, often when you did not really want to. You can also relinquish something you were holding, like a rope or a ball.
explain: Use the clues around the word. «The king relinquished his throne after the war.» A king does not give up his throne for fun, so relinquished must mean gave up. Check it: "The king gave up his throne after the war" still makes sense, so you have the right meaning.
explain: «The climber relinquished her grip and fell.» Here relinquished means let go. Relinquished is a formal word, the sort you meet in history books and grown-up stories. When you see it, think of the simpler words give up or let go.
fact: Relinquish means to give up or let go of something.
fact: Relinquished is the past tense, so it tells you something has already happened.
fact: Relinquished often goes with control, power, a throne, a right or a hold: relinquished control, relinquished the throne, relinquished his hold.
fact: Give up and let go are simpler ways to say relinquish.
fact: Relinquish is a formal word, so you will meet it more in books, news and history than in everyday chat.
exercise: «The king relinquished his throne.» What does relinquished mean here? | *Gave up | Painted | Sat down on | Counted
exercise: «She relinquished her hold on the rope.» What does relinquished mean here? | *Let go of | Pulled harder on | Looked at | Tied tightly
exercise: Which word could replace relinquished in «He relinquished control of the team»? | Rode | *Gave up | Built | Laughed about
test: «The mayor relinquished power after the vote.» What did the mayor do? | Sang a song | Bought a house | *Gave up power | Ran a race
test: «Grandma relinquished her seat on the bus to the old man.» What does relinquished suggest? | Broke the seat | Hid under the seat | *Gave up her seat | Painted the seat
exam: Which is the best meaning of relinquished? | Forgot a word | Won a prize | Held on tightly for ever | *Gave up or let go of something
